The formatDuration function has incorrect pluralization logic. When checking if a value should be plural, the condition uses `> 1`, but the value `1` should use singular form. For example, "1 days" is incorrect - it should be "1 day". The same issue exists for hours, minutes, and seconds. The correct condition should check `!== 1` instead of `> 1`.
```suggestion
  if (days > 0) parts.push(`${days} day${days !== 1 ? "s" : ""}`)
  if (hours % 24 > 0) parts.push(`${hours % 24} hour${hours % 24 !== 1 ? "s" : ""}`)
  if (parts.length === 0 && minutes > 0) parts.push(`${minutes} minute${minutes !== 1 ? "s" : ""}`)
  if (parts.length === 0) parts.push(`${seconds} second${seconds !== 1 ? "s" : ""}`)
```

URL parsing and assignment logic (lines 380-386) creates a parsed URL but doesn't perform any transformation or rewriting. The comment on line 385 mentions "Google AI uses the generativelanguage API endpoint" but the code just assigns parsed to url without modification. This appears to be incomplete implementation - either implement the URL rewriting to point to Google's API endpoint, or remove the misleading comment if URL rewriting is not needed.
```suggestion
            // Google AI uses the generativelanguage API endpoint
            const googleEndpoint = new URL(GOOGLE_AI_API_ENDPOINT)
            if (parsed.hostname !== googleEndpoint.hostname) {
              parsed.protocol = googleEndpoint.protocol
              parsed.host = googleEndpoint.host
              // Ensure the Google API base path (e.g. /v1beta) is present
              const basePath = googleEndpoint.pathname.replace(/\/$/, "")
              if (basePath && !parsed.pathname.startsWith(basePath)) {
                parsed.pathname = `${basePath}${parsed.pathname.startsWith("/") ? "" : "/"}${parsed.pathname}`
              }
            }
```

The OAuth server uses a hardcoded port (1456) without error handling for port conflicts. If port 1456 is already in use by another application, Bun.serve() will throw an error and the OAuth flow will fail with an unclear error message. Consider either: 1) wrapping the Bun.serve() call in a try-catch and providing a helpful error message, 2) implementing port fallback logic to try alternative ports, or 3) using port 0 to let the system assign an available port dynamically.
```suggestion
  try {
    oauthServer = Bun.serve({
      port: OAUTH_PORT,
      fetch(req) {
        const url = new URL(req.url)

        if (url.pathname === "/auth/callback") {
          const code = url.searchParams.get("code")
          const state = url.searchParams.get("state")
          const error = url.searchParams.get("error")
          const errorDescription = url.searchParams.get("error_description")

          if (error) {
            const errorMsg = errorDescription || error
            pendingOAuth?.reject(new Error(errorMsg))
            pendingOAuth = undefined
            return new Response(HTML_ERROR(errorMsg), {
              headers: { "Content-Type": "text/html" },
            })
          }

          if (!code) {
            const errorMsg = "Missing authorization code"
            pendingOAuth?.reject(new Error(errorMsg))
            pendingOAuth = undefined
            return new Response(HTML_ERROR(errorMsg), {
              status: 400,
              headers: { "Content-Type": "text/html" },
            })
          }

          if (!pendingOAuth || state !== pendingOAuth.state) {
            const errorMsg = "Invalid state - potential CSRF attack"
            pendingOAuth?.reject(new Error(errorMsg))
            pendingOAuth = undefined
            return new Response(HTML_ERROR(errorMsg), {
              status: 400,
              headers: { "Content-Type": "text/html" },
            })
          }

          const current = pendingOAuth
          pendingOAuth = undefined

          exchangeCodeForTokens(code, `http://localhost:${OAUTH_PORT}/auth/callback`, current.pkce)
            .then((tokens) => current.resolve(tokens))
            .catch((err) => current.reject(err))

          return new Response(HTML_SUCCESS, {
            headers: { "Content-Type": "text/html" },
          })
        }

        if (url.pathname === "/cancel") {
          pendingOAuth?.reject(new Error("Login cancelled"))
          pendingOAuth = undefined
          return new Response("Login cancelled", { status: 200 })
        }

        return new Response("Not found", { status: 404 })
      },
    })
  } catch (error) {
    log.error("failed to start google oauth server", {
      port: OAUTH_PORT,
      error: error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error),
    })
    throw new Error(
      `Failed to start local OAuth callback server on port ${OAUTH_PORT}. The port may already be in use or blocked.`
    )
  }
```

Header manipulation logic deletes both lowercase 'authorization' and capitalized 'Authorization' headers from the object-style headers (lines 336-337), but HTTP headers are case-insensitive and should be treated as such. This approach misses headers with other capitalizations like 'AUTHORIZATION' or 'Authorization'. Consider normalizing header key comparisons or using a more robust method to ensure the authorization header is removed regardless of capitalization.
```suggestion
                for (const key of Object.keys(init.headers)) {
                  if (key.toLowerCase() === "authorization") {
                    delete init.headers[key as keyof typeof init.headers]
                  }
                }
```

